#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125390} :activity/id #uuid "6257a384-69ce-502a-8a3f-5b199a93b00a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125780 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ invertible matrix $A.$ Remember that if $A$ is invertible, then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has only the trivial solution $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's extract all possible information from this fact using the vector spaces related to the matrix, namely, the *column space* and the *null space*.\n\n- The fact that the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ tells us that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},\n$$\n\n or alternatively, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{nullity}(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Furthermore, if $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=0$ then $A$ has no *non-pivot* columns, or equivalently, all the columns of $A$ are *pivot columns*. So \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n- Finally, since $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its basis has $n$ linearly independent vectors, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nAll the results mentioned above can be summarized in the following theorem, called the **invertible matrix theorem**:\n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n\n$$\nA \\textrm{ is invertible} \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad \\left\\{\\; \\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned} \\right.\n$$\n\n" :tutorial/id #uuid "555ca00c-d37e-5c67-a658-69b087390920" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3522 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4620379b-2eed-5dd7-bf3a-9edf0b4aea79" :step/math-academy-id 19988 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124320 :question/id #uuid "266a9a4e-cb11-5e4a-a2ac-c2e734966372" :question/math-academy-id "e-5424" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, and \n\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{ \\left. \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\;\\right| \\;x_1\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}?\n$$\n\n\n\nI. $A$ is not invertible\n\nII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^4$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following: \n\n\n\n$$\nA \\textrm{ is invertible} \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad \\left\\{\\; \\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={\\mathbb{R}}^{4} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned} \\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since \n\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{ \\left. x_1 \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\;\\right| \\;x_1\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\},\n$$\n\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nSo,  according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the matrix $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Col}(A) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{rank}(A) \\neq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519985} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617034}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "77230797-e80e-5644-8b1e-9e3722a0b66d" :step/math-academy-id 19991 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124433 :question/id #uuid "c1120305-c8cb-5f57-97b1-9b31dc059b4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9961" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\det(A) = 0$\n\nII. $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n\nIII. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible \n\n$$\n\\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad \\left\\{\\; \\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned} \\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\det(A) = 0.$* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nSince $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n.$* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550212} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662344}] :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7ebf299f-89ad-55ae-8fe6-d0af3bc56dfd" :step/math-academy-id 37508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124434 :question/id #uuid "fff1daf8-5a1f-54c9-90d7-87710ca00d23" :question/math-academy-id "e-9962" :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA \\textrm{ is invertible} \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad \\left\\{\\; \\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\} \\\\ & \\bbox[2pt,border:2px solid Silver]{\\bbox[white, 2pt]{\\phantom{WWW}}}=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned} \\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n\n$$\nA \\textrm{ is invertible} \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad \\left\\{\\; \\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\} \\\\ & \\bbox[2pt,border:2px solid Silver]{\\bbox[white, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\text{dim(Null}(A))}}}=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned} \\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nThe missing expression is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543842} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662306}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "debf1d2f-c0e6-5c61-b195-a95710e2c35e" :step/math-academy-id 37509 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125391}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124685}]}
